WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.943
SPEAKER: We're going to take a
look at how Cloud Code makes it

00:00:02.943 --> 00:00:05.360
easy to develop for Kubernetes.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.990
To develop for
Kubernetes, you need

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.380
to set up Kubernetes
configuration for your project,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.070
find how to efficiently iterate
on your code on Kubernetes,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.770
and learn how to perform
common development tasks

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.470
on Kubernetes, like debugging.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.210
But it can be
challenging to master

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.780
all the necessary config, slow
and error-prone to perform

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.240
the repetitive tasks of
building, pushing and deploying

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.360
images, and difficult to set up
debugging against a Kubernetes

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.040
cluster.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.160
That's why we built Cloud Code.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.260
Cloud Code is a set
of development tools

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.570
for Google Cloud that
includes IDE extensions

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.300
for Visual Studio Code,
and JetBrains IDEs,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.370
such as intelliJ.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.320
Cloud Code helps you
author Kubernetes

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.800
config with completions,
linting, inline documentation,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.210
and code snippets.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.370
It automates the process
of running and updating

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.650
your code on Kubernetes
and so much more,

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.070
that we don't have time to
talk about it all today.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.110
To get started with
Cloud Code, download it

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.410
from your favorite IDE's
extension marketplace.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:09.060
Let's take a look at Cloud
Code for VS CODE in action.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.000
We'll get started by using Cloud
Code to create a new Kubernetes

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.350
Project.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.920
Cloud Code also works with your
existing applications, as well.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.710
First, select the Cloud
Code menu from the task bar

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.300
and select New Application.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.490
Next, choose the language
and template you want to use.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:31.480
I'll choose Node js hello world.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.910
Finally, name your project
and hit Enter to create it.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.800
Now that our project
has been created,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.700
let's take a look at
a Kubernetes config.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.900
Expand Kubernetes' manifest
and select helloservice.yaml.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.210
Since this is a
starter application,

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.980
the yaml's already here for us.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.640
But if it wasn't, you could
use Control-Space to view

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.340
the available snippets for
Kubernetes config files

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.960
to create your own from scratch.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.520
Here, let's just see
how Cloud Code helps

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.650
add a new property to the spec.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.890
I can return to the next line
and use Control-Space again

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.510
to see completions for
possible properties.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.640
External traffic policy
looks interesting.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.670
Let's take a look at that.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.560
Cloud Code offers
inline documentation

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.220
here and on hover for all
these Kubernetes types.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.770
After accepting
external traffic policy,

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.160
let's try setting it to none.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.010
Looks like I didn't read the
documentation well enough.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.450
Cloud Code's linting
is telling me

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:44.680
I need to use cluster or local.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.520
Let's use cluster.

00:02:48.742 --> 00:02:51.200
Now that we've seen how Cloud
Code helps manager Kubernetes

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.390
in yaml, let's see how it
helps you deploy to Kubernetes.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.015
First, we need a
cluster to deploy to.

00:02:58.015 --> 00:02:59.640
Let's move over to
the Cloud Code view.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.680
I went ahead and created
a Google Kubernetes engine

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.130
cluster from Cloud Code earlier,
by clicking this button.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.670
But you can add any Kubernetes
cluster, including local ones,

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.880
such as Docker or MiniKube.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.225
To deploy, we select Cloud
Code from the task bar

00:03:20.225 --> 00:03:24.160
again, and select
Run on Kubernetes.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.410
I'll confirm the cluster
I want to deploy to,

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:32.480
confirm a default image registry
for my Google Cloud project.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.150
And Cloud Code begins
the deployment.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.460
It looks like the deployment
has completed successfully.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.900
So let's take a look by Command
clicking the IP address shown

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.860
in the output window.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.050
When we return to Cloud Code,
we see that watch mode is on.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.480
That means I can
make a code change

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.150
and it will automatically
be redeployed.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.360
Let's give that a try.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:04.640
I'll expand the source
folder, select app.js,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.830
and change hello world to
hello devs, and save the file.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.890
We can see that Cloud
Code is automatically

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:13.890
redeploying the service.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:19.240
And we can return
to the web browser,

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:24.760
refresh, and see
the updated page.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.340
Let's see what it looks
like to debug a Kubernetes

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.020
app with Cloud Code.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.180
I'll go to the Run
and debug view.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.310
Select debug on Kubernetes
and click the Play button.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:43.530
We see in the output, that
the debugger is attached.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.410
So let's set a breakpoint
in the app.js file

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:47.370
and reload the page.

00:04:56.437 --> 00:04:58.520
The breakpoint hit, and
we can see all the utility

00:04:58.520 --> 00:04:59.990
that we expect
with the debugger,

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.750
even with code running
on a Kubernetes cluster.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.250
You just learned
how Cloud Code lets

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.530
you focus on writing code
like you always have,

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.960
and spend less time learning
an endless set of new tools

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.690
and technology just to be
productive on Kubernetes.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

